<h2 id="stage2">7. Controlled experiments and source attribution — Stage 02</h2>
<p>The next stage addresses a causal question: does host recruitment depend on the assigned host–graft synapses under the exact settings being tested? It also distinguishes that question from the origin of an extracellular waveform. Read the <a href="/simulations/biohybrid-interface/questions/">34-question scientific decision register</a>, then use the <a href="/simulations/biohybrid-interface/#controlled-experiments">controlled-experiment workbench</a>.</p>
<h3>Matched controls and per-cell interpretation</h3>
<p>The causal audit recalculates three independent runs: the assigned junctions, the identical network with every host–graft peak conductance set to zero, and the assigned network with stimulus amplitude set to zero. Morphology, membrane and axial parameters, temperature, initial condition, electrode placement, resolution and observation window remain matched. The deliberately removed synaptic conductance changes postsynaptic loading. Removing conductance is a mathematical intervention; it does not model a drug, surgical transection, cell death or tissue injury.</p>
<p>A host that fires in the assigned run and loses every somatic spike in the blocked run is labeled “lost with block.” A host that fires in both is “persists with block”; it cannot establish exclusive synaptic mediation. A host that starts firing after conductance removal is “appears with block.” Cells that never fire in either run are “silent in both.” Counts, first-spike times and all host spike times remain in the protocol record. Temporal order alone is still only relay-consistent evidence. These labels apply to the finite observation window and this mathematical network.</p>
<div class="bh-table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Default matched condition</th><th>Host recruitment / cells</th><th>R2 neural / µV p–p</th><th>R2 graft / µV p–p</th><th>R2 host / µV p–p</th></tr></thead><tbody>{[['Assigned junctions',audit.baseline],['Synapses off',audit.synapsesOff],['Zero stimulus',audit.noStimulus]].map(([label,run])=><tr><td>{label}</td><td>{run.metrics.recruitedHosts} / {run.metrics.hostCount}</td><td>{f(run.recordings[1].neuralPeakToPeakUv)}</td><td>{f(run.recordings[1].graftPeakToPeakUv)}</td><td>{f(run.recordings[1].hostPeakToPeakUv)}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div>
<p>The blocked recording remains nonzero while host recruitment is zero. Its small host-source component reflects subthreshold membrane currents. A separate mixed-activation positive control uses a 300 µm tract, stimulation x = 300 µm, −30 µA first phase, one pulse and a 25 ms observation window: all three hosts fire with assigned junctions; host 2 still fires without them, while hosts 1 and 3 lose their spikes. A one-graft/one-host direct-activation control at −40 µA also persists with zero coupling. These are synthetic benchmark counterexamples, not measured graft behavior.</p>
<h3>Known-source decomposition</h3>
<pre>V_neural,k(t) = Σ_i L_ki I_membrane,i(t)
V_graft,k(t)  = Σ_(i in graft) L_ki I_membrane,i(t)
V_host,k(t)   = Σ_(i in host)  L_ki I_membrane,i(t)
V_neural,k(t) = V_graft,k(t) + V_host,k(t)</pre>
<p>The original Float64 membrane currents and the same finite-contact transfer coefficients produce both components. The underlying membrane equations and original combined recording are unchanged. This is a partition of known simulated sources, not an inverse reconstruction or experimental spike-sorting method. The instantaneous waveforms add; separate peak-to-peak values generally do not, because their extrema occur at different times. A host-source field does not establish a host spike or a functional synapse.</p>
<p>The default sample-wise partition residual is {f(stage2.sourceIdentity.maximumResidualUv)} µV across {stage2.sourceIdentity.checkedSamples.toLocaleString('en-US')} contact samples, against a predeclared {stage2.acceptance.sourceIdentityMaximumUv} µV acceptance. Independent native NEURON membrane currents are also partitioned and projected through independently implemented Python transfer coefficients. Maximum graft-source disagreement is {f(sourceMaximum('graftSourceMaximumErrorUv'))} µV; maximum host-source disagreement is {f(sourceMaximum('hostSourceMaximumErrorUv'))} µV, each against an unchanged 0.001 µV electrical criterion.</p>
<h3>Finite parameter studies, dose and reproducibility</h3>
<p>Five study axes vary one setting at a time: signed first-phase current, each synaptic peak conductance, recording height, the shared graft/host axon-diameter parameter or conductivity. Two to seven finite, unique, ordered values are allowed. Paired synapses-off controls are optional for each sample. The workbench reports recruited-cell counts in the listed sample order; these are not population activation probabilities. Adjacent valid samples can bracket a response change. No interpolation, monotonic threshold search, Lapicque fit or untested extrapolation is performed. Failures retain their actual settings and error message and break response intervals.</p>
<p>Moving a passive recording disk leaves membrane dynamics unchanged. Changing conductivity alters both stimulation and recording, so those response studies are not isolated inverse-conductivity tests. The diameter parameter applies to the engineered tract and host axons; graft terminal branches use 0.8 times that diameter. Changing it alters axial conductance and membrane area together in both populations, so downstream changes cannot be attributed solely to the engineered tract. Each case starts from the same equilibrium and explicit base settings; it is not a continuation from the previous case.</p>
<p>A separate refinement check recalculates the observed current-response bracket endpoints at 0.005 and 0.0025 ms with all other settings fixed. At −12 µA, both steps recruit zero hosts; at −18 µA, both recruit three hosts, with one somatic spike per host. The largest event-index-matched host spike-time change is {f(boundaryTimingMaximum,6)} ms. The frozen acceptance checks recruitment and per-host spike counts; timing changes are reported without a new timing tolerance. This supports these two sampled endpoints, not an exact threshold or uniform convergence of every value between them.</p>
<pre>Q_first [nC] = I_first [µA] × phase duration [ms]
Q_absolute,phase = |Q_first|
A_disk [µm²] = π r²
D_geometric [µC/cm²] = Q_absolute,phase [nC] × 10⁵ / A_disk [µm²]</pre>
<p>Equal opposite phases give zero prescribed net charge, but charge balance does not establish electrochemical or tissue safety. Nominal density uses the geometric disk area; electrode material, impedance, polarization, reaction kinetics and damage are not solved. The default numerical example is {f(audit.baseline.dose.absolutePhaseChargeNc)} nC per phase and {f(audit.baseline.dose.absolutePhaseChargeDensityUcPerCm2)} µC/cm² per phase. It is not a device operating recommendation.</p>
<p>Protocol workers run at most 14 solves, 120 million node-steps and eight million saved voltage values per solve. Excessive requests are rejected before calculation; the solver resolution is never reduced silently. Cancellation terminates the worker. A new run cannot overwrite a later request, and retained results keep their completed settings when controls change. Protocol JSON contains each normalized setting, intervention, host event, source amplitude, unit and failure. CSV contains a row per requested condition; failed measurements remain blank. “Inspect run” recalculates its exact settings for the main anatomical replay.</p>
<h3>Verification and a retained storage-precision failure</h3>
<p>{stage2.checks.filter(check=>check.passed).length} of {stage2.checks.length} quantified Stage 02 acceptance studies pass, including causal loss, zero-input monitored events, mixed and direct activation, source identity, all five default study grids, explicit placement-error retention and the analytical storage-precision check. Dedicated automated tests additionally exercise deterministic repeatability, control matching, malformed protocols, work budgets, cancellation message contracts and export corruption.</p>
<p><strong class="bh-failed">Retained failed supplemental diagnostic:</strong> reconstructing source currents from the saved Float32 voltage movie misses a provisional fixed 0.001 µV absolute criterion for the host source: maximum error {f(stage2.storagePrecision.populations.host.maximumErrorUv)} µV. The graft error is {f(stage2.storagePrecision.populations.graft.maximumErrorUv)} µV. This failure remains in the report. The exported quantitative source waveforms use their original Float64 calculations.</p>
<p>A separate independent calculation propagates each stored voltage’s Float32 half-ULP through the axial-current matrix and recording transfer matrix. All {stage2.storagePrecision.checkedPopulationContactSamples.toLocaleString('en-US')} population/contact samples lie within that analytical quantization bound, with maximum error-to-bound ratio {f(stage2.storagePrecision.maximumErrorToBoundRatio)}. Passing this different, physically justified storage-precision check does not turn the failed absolute diagnostic into a pass. The native-source comparison above checks the original quantitative waveforms separately.</p>
<h3>Retained cross-runtime exact-literal failure</h3>
<p><strong class="bh-failed">Two original exact-literal regression comparisons failed in GitHub CI.</strong> Peak-to-peak values saved under Node 24.19.0 were not bit-identical under Node 22.23.3: R2 was 9.745329941385737 versus 9.745329941385254 µV, a difference of approximately 4.83 × 10⁻¹³ µV. The raw values and failed job remain in the release verification record. This was a failed cross-runtime bit-equality assumption; it was not a failed native-reference or convergence criterion.</p>
<p>The replacement regression compares every original voltage, trace, recording and event exactly against the frozen released solver in the same runtime, with pinned source provenance and dependencies. The worker transport test likewise requires exact equality to a direct calculation in that runtime. Neither check adds an error allowance, and the independent native-reference and physical convergence tolerances remain unchanged. <a href="https://tc39.es/ecma262/multipage/numbers-and-dates.html#sec-math.exp">ECMAScript permits implementation-approximated transcendental functions</a>; cross-runtime bit identity is not assumed. These measurements do not isolate a particular built-in operation as the cause of the observed difference.</p>
<p>The source-hashed report and reproduction script are retained as <code>docs/biohybrid/stage2-validation.json</code> and <code>scripts/validate-biohybrid-stage2.mjs</code>. Initial Node measurements were approximately {f(stage2.timing.measurements.find(item=>item.id==='default-audit').elapsedMs/1000,3)} seconds for the three-run default audit; browser, device, population and resolution determine actual elapsed time.</p>

<h2 id="growth">8. Growth on a separate timescale</h2>
<p>The growth module is a seeded geometric model over days. It combines persistent direction, a target-directed cue, stochastic steering, branching, cylinder-wall reflections, target-sphere encounters, a formation trial and a maturation delay. It does not run on the electrical replay clock and does not reproduce the concentration-based model of <a href="#marinov2020">Marinov et al.</a></p>
<p>Branching uses p = 1 − exp(−λ Δt), with λ in day⁻¹. This preserves the constant-hazard survival probability across step subdivisions; the full branching process is still an approximation because at most one child is created per path per step, at the step endpoint. Segment/sphere intersection checks an entire extension, avoiding missed targets between endpoints. Each distinct path–target encounter gets one formation trial. An accepted encounter becomes a candidate only after an assumed maturation interval; every candidate remains explicitly unvalidated for function. Seeded steering, branching and formation streams are separate, so changing formation probability does not move the existing trajectories.</p>
<p>The default elongation rate of 180 µm/day, cue strength, branching hazard, formation probability and maturation interval are exploratory estimates. Published micro-TENN growth rates depend strongly on preparation and culture stage. The laboratory does not turn a culture observation into an in-vivo integration forecast. The default realization can encounter targets without forming any candidate synapses.</p>
<p>Only an explicit button transfers the number of candidate host targets to pending electrical settings. This transfers a count, not the identity, trajectory or individual weight of each candidate connection. The user then runs a separate electrical calculation with the assigned synaptic conductance. The grown geometry is not substituted for the electrical cable mesh. A path cap suppresses additional branches and reports that limitation; a vertex cap stops the calculation at the last completed step and reports the truncated horizon. Growth tests establish deterministic and geometric consistency, not experimental calibration.</p>

<h2>9. Reproduction and release limits</h2>
<pre>node scripts/validate-biohybrid-stage2.mjs
node --test tests/biohybrid-engine.mjs tests/biohybrid-export.mjs \
  tests/biohybrid-growth.mjs tests/biohybrid-reference.mjs \
  tests/biohybrid-protocols.mjs tests/biohybrid-sources.mjs \
  tests/biohybrid-protocol-export.mjs tests/biohybrid-workers.mjs \
  tests/biohybrid-response-boundary.mjs
node scripts/validate-biohybrid.mjs
node scripts/biohybrid/reference-input.mjs /tmp/biohybrid-input.json.gz
python scripts/biohybrid/neuron-reference.py \
  /tmp/biohybrid-input.json.gz docs/biohybrid/neuron-validation.json
npm run build</pre>
<p>The independent reference dependency is pinned to NEURON 9.0.1. The repository retains the scientific architecture, numerical report, native comparison, growth methods and validation history. The WebGL scene uses calculated compartment voltage, while the 2D fallback uses the same data and color map. The verification browser had WebGL disabled: the fallback, computation and interaction path were visually checked; a GPU-enabled browser remains necessary for a direct visual check of the 3D view.</p>
<p>Remaining scientific work includes measured graft-cell morphologies and channel fits; temperature/preparation-specific calibration; experimental stimulus and recording comparisons; heterogeneous scaffold/tissue conductivities; electrode impedance and noise; inhibition, adaptation, plasticity and synaptic variability; validated growth-to-connectivity mapping; and empirically calibrated long-term changes. The degradation preset is a sensitivity study, not a prediction of implantation lifetime.</p>

